# UI rules

## The symbol invariant

Anywhere a ticker is shown — timeline, watchlist, ribbon, themes popup,
scanner, flagged list — it must support all three:

1. **Flag / unflag** (spacebar, or click the flag)
2. **Right-click → add to watchlist** (`components/SymbolContextMenu.tsx`)
3. **AHK fire** on click or spacebar (`lib/ahk.ts`)

This is a standing requirement from the user, not a nice-to-have. A new
surface that shows symbols without these is incomplete.

## Theming

Five themes: `dark` (default), `blue`, `wallst`, `crimson`, `nebula` — all
dark. Defined as CSS custom properties in `app/globals.css`.

**Always use the variables**, never a hardcoded color:

    var(--bg-base)  var(--bg-glass)      var(--border-glass)
    var(--text-primary)  var(--text-secondary)  var(--text-muted)
    var(--accent-primary)  var(--accent-glow)

A literal hex in a component looks fine in the default theme and breaks in the
other four. The exception is alert-type color, which is deliberately inline hex
(see the alerts rule).

Reusable classes already exist — `.glass-panel`, `.glass-card`,
`.glass-header`, `.glass-input`, `.glass-btn`, `.btn-primary`. Use them rather
than rebuilding the glass treatment.

## Density

This is a trading terminal, not a marketing page. Rows are 26px in the alert
bar and deliberately tight elsewhere. Do not add padding, rounding or
whitespace "for readability" — screen real estate is the scarce resource, and
users are scanning many rows at once.

## Pop-out windows

`app/pop/*` render the same components in separate browser windows, synced via
BroadcastChannel (`hooks/useCrossWindowSync.ts`). A component that reads from
the store works in a pop-out automatically; one holding local state does not.
